-- title:
--   Executing one complete random graph-sketch trial
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a graph on $n$ vertices, $k\ge1$, and $\omega$ a full trial bit block. In a ready native graph state, suppose the coin stack begins with the corresponding $T=nk+kn+(k-1)k^2n$ signs. The trial program consumes exactly those signs and prepends the nonnegative integer
--   $$Z=\operatorname{trialNumerator}(\operatorname{walkTensor}(A,k),\omega)$$
--   to the norm-sum register. Every other native register, input position, output, and coin tail is restored. It runs within $\operatorname{nativeTrialCost}(n,k,|A|)$, the explicit sum of sign loading, first-layer construction, later-layer driving, norm computation, and cleanup bounds. This is the native realization of one squared final sketch norm.
-- source:
--   T. Koana, Approximate Counting of k-Paths in O*(2^k) Time, STOC 2027 submission #953, October 2026, §3.2 (printed pp. 8–9 / PDF pp. 9–10) and §4.1 (printed p. 9 / PDF p. 10), https://github.com/sigact-stoc/stoc2027/releases/download/papers-2/953.pdf. Native implementation: Solutions/NativeTrial.lean, line 24; source snapshot SHA-256 50bb005f4a68f81ad113ff32d2174f9061b9217fd8b5d4bbe827c3017411e5b4.

